 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1031 "Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1033 942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049755 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049918 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049918 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049918 #edb/ref 1031 "Closure Properties of Rational Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049918 #edb/ref 1033 2039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049918 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1e621c0b-a301-5a53-b49d-32f7ecc750b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1031 "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using Polynomial Factorization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1033 1676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050323 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3fe5d92e-4a94-51cb-9e17-50ec23a535d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1031 "Further Factoring of Polynomials Using GCFs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1033 2337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050827 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "69d60d04-3212-57d7-b613-ac51716a407a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\n- $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\n- $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091946 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091947 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\n- $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091948 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092159 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186092159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092312 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092327 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092328 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092329 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1045 "Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092437 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e6f89105-7e4b-5912-9b01-20e1a6810bb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093195 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093195 #edb/ref 1061 37591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1dac5f5-12ea-56be-8067-1b9f815159f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1061 37594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e843dd4-2f8a-5237-87b2-28b5a09adb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1061 37596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82947aff-7f67-51bc-9a88-6e5d204e1c08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1061 37829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2236e99f-a53e-57c2-91ab-c3091e8a86a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1061 37830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b4c7081-0037-5d22-8c22-bf18957c01d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1061 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90c92812-0369-53f2-8139-3a602c9d8ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1061 4198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11713b46-54cf-58b5-8dc5-2984d413ed5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1061 4201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd94fe81-ec77-53b8-8ef8-e461ac344d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1061 4204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cabcbccd-aabb-5b9e-a421-ce222693f535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1064 "When we add, subtract, or multiply two integers, the answer is always an integer. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two integers:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na + b = 3+2 = 5\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na - b = 3-2 = 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- However, dividing the two integers does *not* give an integer:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac32 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nWe say the integers are **closed** under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but **not closed** under division.\n\n**Watch Out!** Although integers are not closed under division, the ratio of two integers is *sometimes* an integer. For example, if $a=4$ and $b=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} =  \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2,\n$$\n\nwhich is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1065 1593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7d0a253-b58a-5205-b05d-d5d2f1d49f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n> *The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n> $$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1065 6201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60187490-7cd6-566d-ab45-128e2c4f81c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1067 "The Degree of a Product of Two Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the degree of the sum and difference of two polynomials $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nFirst, let's suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have *different* degrees. In this case, the degree of the sum or difference equals the largest of the two degrees. We write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) = \\max \\big\\{ \\textrm{deg}(P), \\textrm{deg}(Q) \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $\\textrm{max}$ function simply returns the largest element of the set.\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\textrm{deg}(P) = 5$ and $\\textrm{deg}(Q) = 2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P + Q) = \\max \\big\\{ 5, 2 \\big\\} = 5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why this is true, note that the leading term of $P$ contains an $x^5,$ and the leading term of $Q$ has an $x^2.$ When we add the two polynomials, no term of $Q$ could cancel out the leading term of $P.$ So the sum of the polynomials must have degree $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1065 6202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fae91d47-b786-5c2f-9a29-dfb06624930f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1064 "Polynomials are also closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but not closed under division.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = x+1, \\qquad Q(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =(x+1)+({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =(x+1)-({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\cdot Q & =(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2}+1\\cdot {x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- However, *dividing* the two polynomials does *not* give a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{P}{Q} & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Although polynomials are not closed under division, the ratio of two polynomials is *sometimes* a polynomial. For example, if $P=x^2$ and $Q=x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P}{Q} =  \\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1065 6228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d529e51a-742c-52f5-874b-546cf98a1849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1067 "Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1064 "Now suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have the *same* degree. That is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) =   \\textrm{deg}(Q) = n.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, the degree of the sum or difference can be *any integer* from $0$ to $n.$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) \\leq n\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Let $P$ and $Q$ be polynomials of degree $15.$ Then, the *largest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $15$ since the resulting polynomial can't contain terms of order higher than $15.$\n\n- On the other hand, the *smallest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $0.$ For instance, suppose $P=x^{15}$ and $Q=1-x^{15}.$ Then $P +Q = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1065 6229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9d9fe12-e873-5b5b-aca8-f212f9be62b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269380 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269913 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46da9874-30bc-583c-a863-5aae9560a9cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270065 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270065 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270065 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59a1700f-ccf6-5e35-9e43-a7880fc832ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270310 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270310 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270310 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "75df7d2e-780f-56cd-92ad-5720fda4a706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271001 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271001 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271001 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c456bd0a-afad-5d98-8f69-257b595ed130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271765 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "903f2292-1c52-539d-84aa-b1a6a93b0960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272247 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
